module bindings.c.stack_fallback_stride_underalloc;

// The dual-path buffer idiom keeps a fixed stack array as the common-case
// scratch buffer -- declared with the product of a count bound and a per-unit
// width -- aliases it to a pointer, and falls back to a heap allocation only
// when a count exceeds the array's count bound. The write loop states the
// width again as the stride the buffer pointer advances by per row, so the
// fallback allocation has to size count*width elements. One that takes the
// count alone holds count elements and every heap-path row past the first
// writes past it.
//
// The fact is emitted by the C frontend
// (ccStackFallbackStrideUnderallocObservations,
// internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/c.go) from the syntax tree: the count
// is a size argument the surrounding guard bounds by one factor of the
// array's declared product, and the stride is the identifier a
// buffer-aliased pointer advances by in a compound assignment, tracked
// through reassignment so a pointer that later points elsewhere stops
// contributing. Prose and identifiers unrelated to those pairings cannot
// satisfy them, and naming the stride in any size argument -- including a
// different width variable, as a column-resolved variant legitimately does
// -- clears it.
binding stackFallbackStrideUnderalloc {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.kind == "any" and node.analysis == "alloc.stack_fallback_stride_underalloc"
  emit issue code.StackFallbackStrideUnderallocation at node
}
